<statement>
<p>
Consider the following algorithm to add two one-dimensional arrays together.</p>
<p>
def array1d<underscore/>sum(myArray1, myArray2): </p>
<p>
  n1 = length(myArray1) </p>
<p>
  n2 = length(myArray2) </p>
<p>
  if(n1 not equal n2): </p>
<p>
    print("Warning: Arrays not same length!") </p>
<p>
  sumArrays = <lbracket/><rbracket/> </p>
<p>
  for i in <lbracket/>0, 1, ..., n1 - 1<rbracket/>: </p>
<p>
    sumArrays<lbracket/>i<rbracket/> = myArray1<lbracket/>i<rbracket/> + myArray2<lbracket/>i<rbracket/> </p>
<p>
  return sumArrays
</p>
<p>
What is the run-time complexity of the alrorithm, using big-O notation? </p>
<p><ol style="list-style-type:upper-alpha"><li><m>\displaystyle{O}{\left({n}\right)}</m></li> 
<li><m>\displaystyle{O}{\left({n}^{{2}}\right)}</m></li> 
<li><m>\displaystyle{O}{\left({\log{{\left({n}\right)}}}\right)}</m></li> 
<li><m>\displaystyle{O}{\left({1}\right)}</m></li> 
<li><m>\displaystyle{O}{\left({n}\cdot{\log{{\left({n}\right)}}}\right)}</m></li> 
</ol></p>
 

</statement>
<solution>
<p><ul><li>A: <m>\displaystyle{O}{\left({n}\right)}</m></li></ul></p>
</solution>
</myopenmath>

